{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791441592,"nonce":"UgbWPFyp/yDjvOdvNewdwo9Abx4=","title":"Cranelift: sinking arithmetic past allocation doubles live FP operands and spills","message":"Lua op_overload lua$main$3 contains a typed vector-add arm. Input CLIF loads six f64 operands, computes three fadd results, calls the 72-byte allocator, then stores the three results in the new object. Generated x86 code instead spills all six inputs across the allocation call, reloads all six and performs the additions afterwards. Six live floating-point values cross the call where the source CLIF requires only three results. All six spills use 16-byte movdqu stores and loads.\n\nThis is an observed change between input CLIF and native code; the responsible optimization/lowering phase has not yet been isolated. It does not establish that scheduling caused the previously observed between-build timing modes in 27d96d2c7a0dfd477a554f3cfc872182b9ff0248ce87c5773bc985b4592eec38. The source HIR also retains two temporary vector allocations per iteration; guarded scalar replacement is a separate, larger opportunity.\n\nCreate a minimal Cranelift reproducer and inspect the pinned Wasmtime optimizer/lowering to locate where fadd is sunk across the call. Evaluate a pressure-aware scheduling/materialization rule rather than globally disabling optimization. Keep load ordering and FP behavior intact. Pin the native spill count/code shape for this case, then compare LLVM-off op_overload and the other call-heavy kernels on both architectures. Measure compile time too; the purpose is better Cranelift without LLVM promotion overhead.\n\nEvidence: op_overload/extracted/lua_main_3-0.clif lines 567-600 versus lua_main_3-0.vcode lines 401-438. Similar typed arms have the same pattern.\n\nCaptured on the NUC (i5-1250P, x86-64) using saved fdf137cb52c3dc468a8c52d5bc88b1a6431a3a1c binaries, LLVM disabled, normal interpreter-first tiering. No builds ran. This is code-quality evidence, not a measured speedup. ZYNTAX_DUMP_CRANELIFT is input CLIF, before Cranelift optimization; the paired VCode dump is generated native code. OSR tracing was enabled, so total function call counts include instrumentation and cold paths.\n\nArtifacts: NUC /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit-fdf137cb and local /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit-fdf137cb-local. Script /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit.py. Local extracted/*.clif and *.vcode split the named dumps from stderr.log; optimized HIR is in each case's hir directory.","files":null}]}